./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:578:141:## 7. The dispatcher and runs
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:581:249:**Build in this order**: (1) contracts skeleton — schema DDL, enums, typed models, adapter interfaces, config loading, CLI/API surface stubs; (2) **the verify instrument** (18.2), runnable with every checklist item FAIL; (3) pure-logic modules + unit tests (items 1–21 and 36) green; (4) server wiring — API, WS, resolution engine over DB, dispatcher, boundary scheduler, CLI against the API; (5) UI views; (6) Playwright e2e suite, items 22–34 green; (7) the fake-ticket dogfood pass (Section 18.5): Level A green (item 35), Levels B and C executed with evidence recorded in DOGFOOD.md. Do not start a later stage while an earlier stage's tests fail.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:583:265:- Unit items are 1–21 and 36; end-to-end items are 22–35. Item 34 (snapshot migration) runs against `migration/source-snapshot/`, and item 35 (dogfood Level A) is the scripted walkthrough — both part of the suite, not manual steps. Levels B and C of the dogfood (Section 18.5) are deliberately OUTSIDE `./verify`: they use live agents and belong to stage 7's evidence, not the deterministic gate.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:586:314:35. Dogfood Level A: `scripts/dogfood_cli.py` drives the full workflow against a test server seeded with `plan seed --demo` — agent actions through the real CLI, human actions (accepts with grant pairs, needs_review approve, day-plan decisions) through the HTTP API, since the CLI deliberately has no human verbs: create a ticket; propose and accept through every gate — every accept carrying its grant pair — including one edit-accept and one superseded proposal; write and overwrite the recap; assign to the day and remove; create a blocking link and confirm dispatch-ineligibility, then complete the blocker and confirm eligibility; claim via the test dispatcher tick; propose result with claim env; approve from needs_review to done. The script asserts each step's resulting state via `--json`/response output and exits non-zero on the first mismatch.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:645:   105	- **Pickup queue** (dispatcher eligibility): Section 7.2.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:661:   121	Runs once per planning date, at the first scheduler tick at/after the boundary hour (and never twice for the same date; a `boundary_runs` table records completions). It has a deterministic pass (pure code) and a judgment pass (agent adapter):
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:678:   138	- Invalidating the root sets root and every child `invalidated` and triggers one replan-adapter call that returns a complete new tree (replacing the old; old tree preserved in the event payload). Invalidating a child replans only that node: the adapter returns a replacement child. Replans serialize; if an invalidation arrives while a replan is in flight, the in-flight result is discarded and one new replan runs (latest wins) [RULING R5 default].
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:681:   141	## 7. The dispatcher and runs
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:685:   145	Every 60s (configurable), inside the server process. Order within a tick: (1) reclaim expired claims and detect dead worker processes; (2) recompute eligibility; (3) spawn up to the concurrency cap. A machine-wide advisory file lock (`data/dispatcher.lock`) guarantees a single dispatcher; a `dispatch_enabled` config flag is re-read every tick and fails safe to `false` on config read error.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:689:   149	A ticket is eligible iff ALL of: not `done`/`dropped`/`needs_review`; not blocked (3.6); not `auto_blocked`; no active claim; and an agent can do work on it — meaning the current gating field has no pending proposal AND either (a) the advance-target ≤ `ceiling` (the agent can move it), or (b) the ticket is at its ceiling with `at_cap = propose` (the agent can draft the gating field for approval). A ticket at its ceiling with `at_cap = stop` is never eligible. Ordering: priority (P0 first), then deadline ascending with NULLs last, then `created_at` ascending.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:691:   151	### 7.3 Claims and runs
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:693:   153	- Claim = CAS: `UPDATE tickets SET claim_lock=?, claim_expires=? WHERE id=? AND claim_lock IS NULL`; rowcount 0 = lost. TTL 15 minutes. Every claim inserts a `runs` row (`ticket_id`, `status` ∈ {running, done, blocked, crashed, timed_out, reclaimed}, `started_at`, `ended_at`, `summary`, `error`, `pid`).
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:694:   154	- Heartbeat (CLI verb) extends `claim_expires` by one TTL. Expired claim or dead PID → run closed `reclaimed`, lock cleared.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:695:   155	- Max concurrent runs: 2 [RULING R7 default]. Per-run max runtime: 30 minutes → SIGTERM, run `timed_out`.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:699:   159	Through a **spawn adapter** (subprocess boundary, mockable): `hermes -p <profile> --skills <skill> chat -q "work planning ticket <id>"`, detached session, per-run log file under `data/logs/`, env: `PLAN_SERVER_URL`, `PLAN_TICKET_ID`, `PLAN_RUN_ID`, `PLAN_CLAIM`. Profile and skill names come from config [RULING R3 default: profile `default`, skill `planning-worker`]. Tests never spawn real processes.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:701:   161	### 7.5 Circuit breaker
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:703:   163	A run ending `crashed`, `timed_out`, or `spawn_failed` increments `tickets.consecutive_failures`; a run ending `done` resets it to 0. Reaching 2 sets `auto_blocked = true` (sticky; event `auto_blocked`). The dispatcher never picks up an auto-blocked ticket; a human clear-action resets both flag and counter.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:707:   167	Every agent write (proposal, recap, heartbeat, run close) must carry `PLAN_RUN_ID` + `PLAN_CLAIM` matching the ticket's active claim, except proposal/recap writes from non-dispatched agent contexts (no claim env — e.g. the planner-main chat agent), which are permitted with the proposer taken from `PLAN_ACTOR` (default `agent`). Human writes happen through the UI/API only — the CLI is an agent tool. A write with a stale/foreign claim is rejected with a structured error naming the mismatch.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:711:   171	A single entry point (`plan`, installed via `pip install -e .` console script) speaking HTTP to the server. **The CLI is for agents (and developer debugging); the human never uses it — every human action lives in the UI.** No resolution verbs exist in it: accepting, approving, granting, unblocking, and day-plan decisions are UI/API-only. Global behavior: `--json` on every verb (machine output; stable exit codes: 0 success, 1 validation/domain error, 2 connection error); long text via `--body-file <path>` or `-` for stdin — body text is never passed as an inline argument; env defaults `PLAN_SERVER_URL`, `PLAN_TICKET_ID` (verbs taking a ticket id use it when omitted), `PLAN_RUN_ID`, `PLAN_CLAIM`, `PLAN_ACTOR`.
