./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6020:SPEC.md:159:Through a **spawn adapter** (subprocess boundary, mockable): `hermes -p <profile> --skills <skill> chat -q "work planning ticket <id>"`, detached session, per-run log file under `data/logs/`, env: `PLAN_SERVER_URL`, `PLAN_TICKET_ID`, `PLAN_RUN_ID`, `PLAN_CLAIM`. Profile and skill names come from config [RULING R3 default: profile `default`, skill `planning-worker`]. Tests never spawn real processes.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6021:SPEC.md:167:Every agent write (proposal, recap, heartbeat, run close) must carry `PLAN_RUN_ID` + `PLAN_CLAIM` matching the ticket's active claim, except proposal/recap writes from non-dispatched agent contexts (no claim env — e.g. the planner-main chat agent), which are permitted with the proposer taken from `PLAN_ACTOR` (default `agent`). Human writes happen through the UI/API only — the CLI is an agent tool. A write with a stale/foreign claim is rejected with a structured error naming the mismatch.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6022:SPEC.md:171:A single entry point (`plan`, installed via `pip install -e .` console script) speaking HTTP to the server. **The CLI is for agents (and developer debugging); the human never uses it — every human action lives in the UI.** No resolution verbs exist in it: accepting, approving, granting, unblocking, and day-plan decisions are UI/API-only. Global behavior: `--json` on every verb (machine output; stable exit codes: 0 success, 1 validation/domain error, 2 connection error); long text via `--body-file <path>` or `-` for stdin — body text is never passed as an inline argument; env defaults `PLAN_SERVER_URL`, `PLAN_TICKET_ID` (verbs taking a ticket id use it when omitted), `PLAN_RUN_ID`, `PLAN_CLAIM`, `PLAN_ACTOR`.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6023:SPEC.md:185:- REST JSON under `/api/`: CRUD + actions mirroring Section 8 verbs, PLUS the human actions that deliberately have no CLI form — accept/edit-accept with the grant pair, `needs_review` approve, unblock, grant changes, sprint create/freeze, day-plan accept/accept-all/invalidate/reject-all, drop — and `GET /api/board`, `GET /api/day/<date>`, `GET /api/sprint/current`, `GET /api/queues`. Every mutating endpoint routes through the same canonical functions (one writer per edge).
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6025:SPEC.md:239:Defaults the owner may override before the run; each is tagged where it binds: **R1** boundary hour 05:00. **R2** new-ticket grant `ceiling=needs_success, at_cap=propose`. **R3** spawn profile `default`, worker skill `planning-worker`. **R4** boundary evidence DB-internal only. **R5** replan serialization, latest-wins. **R6** seed excludes archives. **R7** max concurrent runs 2.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6027:SPEC.md:257:- **DOCS.md** — plain-language documentation written progressively as each stage completes, never retrofitted. A smart non-engineer must be able to read it: what the planner is, the entities, how proposals and ceilings work, what the dispatcher does, how a day flows, what each screen shows, what the CLI verbs do, what the skills are for. If a section needs the code to be understood, rewrite the section.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6029:SPEC.md:314:35. Dogfood Level A: `scripts/dogfood_cli.py` drives the full workflow against a test server seeded with `plan seed --demo` — agent actions through the real CLI, human actions (accepts with grant pairs, needs_review approve, day-plan decisions) through the HTTP API, since the CLI deliberately has no human verbs: create a ticket; propose and accept through every gate — every accept carrying its grant pair — including one edit-accept and one superseded proposal; write and overwrite the recap; assign to the day and remove; create a blocking link and confirm dispatch-ineligibility, then complete the blocker and confirm eligibility; claim via the test dispatcher tick; propose result with claim env; approve from needs_review to done. The script asserts each step's resulting state via `--json`/response output and exits non-zero on the first mismatch.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6031:SPEC.md:329:Live Hermes gateway chat; live dispatcher spawning a real agent; the final cutover seed against the live `~/.hermes/planning/` at switch time (the in-run migration proves the machinery on the frozen snapshot; the live directory will have drifted since 2026-07-04 and its import is reviewed by the human); installing skills into `~/.hermes/skills/`; launchd supervision; UI look-and-feel judgment.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6033:PROGRESS.md:49:Stage 6: dispatch T18 (e2e harness + items 22–27, Fable planner) → when its conftest lands, T19 (items 28–32) ∥ T20 (items 33–34). Then delegated verify (expect 35/36 — item 35 needs T21), stage-6 commit. Stage 7: T21 (dogfood script + four skills docs) closes item 35 → verify 36/36; then Levels B/C run TOP-LEVEL with live hermes (evidence into DOGFOOD.md; staleness rule: rerun if server/CLI/dispatcher code changes after). Then final sequence in ONE turn: fresh ./verify full output → codex audit (codex exec --model gpt-5.6 with codex-audit.md contents) → PASS or fix-loop. DOCS.md needs stage-6/7 sections + a CLI-verbs/skills summary before the audit. Known hazards: codex exec hangs (kill + relaunch tighter); orchestrators idle mid-pipeline (ping them); session restarts kill sub-agents (respawn closers against inherited state); e2e anchored-test 1:1 fence. Anomaly (historic): an unowned codex process ran the AUDIT prompt before the 13:00 restart — void, ignore.
./orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6035:DOCS.md:40:- **Stage 4 — the server (done):** the rules now answer the door. One local process serves a JSON interface for every action in the system, and it enforces who may do what: requests from a working agent must carry a valid claim on the ticket they're touching (a stale or borrowed claim is refused, and the refusal names what was wrong without ever revealing the live claim itself); the actions reserved for the human — approving, granting, unblocking, dropping, freezing, deciding the day plan — refuse agents entirely. A live feed announces every change so screens can refresh themselves; the feed is only a doorbell, never the data. The dispatcher and the morning boundary job now actually run on their schedules (in testing they run only when explicitly ticked, so tests are exact). The command-line tool agents use is complete: every verb talks to the server, returns clean machine-readable output, and deliberately contains no approval powers at all. Four review findings worth naming, all caught by the independent reviewer and fixed: a path that leaked the live claim token into an error detail; a check that ran validation before verifying the claim; a number that could sneak into a date field untyped; and an environment value that could trick the tool into reading the wrong input.
