./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5655:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:357:# upgrading mid-stream before the migration script runs.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5656:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:646:     4	**Contracts read:** decisions.md D11 (binding component inventory — items 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 17 only); PRINCIPLES.md design-system section; SPEC §9 (WS = invalidation signal only, debounce 250ms), §10 (six screens, simplicity ruling, token-file rules), §14 (audio-capture seam), §15 (non-goals); T01 plan §13 (route table + route ownership); the live JSON producers on disk: `src/planner/tickets/api.py` + `views.py`, `src/planner/sprints/api.py` + `views.py`, `src/planner/days/api.py`, `src/planner/dispatch/api.py`, `src/planner/chat/api.py`, `src/planner/core/errors.py` (`to_payload()` → `{"error":{code,message,detail}}`), `src/planner/core/server.py` (meta keys `ui_debounce_ms`/`ws_poll_ms`/`test_mode`; `_SHELL`; `/assets` mount at cwd-relative `assets/`), `src/planner/core/ws.py` (`{events:[{id,entity_id,kind,payload,created_at}...], cursor}` non-empty batches, server poll `ws_poll_ms`), `src/planner/core/config.py` (ui_debounce_ms 250, ws_poll_ms 300, both env-overridable — `PLAN_WS_POLL_MS` exists at config.py:147), `assets/tokens.css` starter.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5657:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1013:   371	2. Temp dir; pick a free port (T10's `free_or` helper). Build an **explicit env dict** (never mutate `os.environ`) and `config = load_config(path=None, env=env)` with: `PLAN_TEST_MODE=1`, `PLAN_FAKE_NOW=2026-07-04T12:00:00`, `PLAN_DB_PATH=<tmp>/planning.db`, `PLAN_PORT=<port>`, `PLAN_LOGS_DIR=<tmp>/logs`, `PLAN_DISPATCHER_LOCK_PATH=<tmp>/dispatcher.lock`, and `PLAN_WS_POLL_MS=50` (config.py:147 — tightens batch cadence so the coalescing proof is timing-robust; ui_debounce_ms stays the default 250 so check (b) asserts the real value).
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5658:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1067:   425	10. **Smoke tempo**: server runs with `PLAN_WS_POLL_MS=50` so the two-event coalescing proof has ~230ms of slack; `ui_debounce_ms` stays 250 (asserted). Residual flake risk only if two localhost POSTs straddle >200ms — accepted; if it ever flakes, the fallback is widening the sleep, not weakening the exactly-one assertion. Reconnect/backoff is implemented per §3 but not smoke-tested (needs a server bounce) — Codex review should eyeball that path.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5659:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1113:    27	With PLAN_TEST_MODE=1 the dispatcher and boundary scheduler do not tick on timers; `POST /api/test/tick-dispatcher` / `tick-boundary` run single synchronous ticks. Deterministic tests require exactly-one-tick semantics; the spec's test endpoints exist precisely to drive ticks.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5661:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1121:    35	From stage 3 onward, each ticket is dispatched to a per-ticket orchestrator sub-agent (Fable) that runs the pipeline internally: planning agent → codex plan review → orchestrator sense-check/adjust → implementation agent → codex diff review → report. The top-level agent only decomposes, integrates serially, runs `./verify`, and spot-checks load-bearing code (resolution engine, claim/reclaim, planning-date math, seed parser per CLAUDE.md). Directed by the owner during the run; supersedes the flat pipeline used for T01/T02 (which ran plan and implementation agents directly under the top-level orchestrator).
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5662:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1123:    37	Model tiering (owner: use judgement, Fable allowed liberally for groundwork): planners are Fable for the load-bearing groundwork tickets — T04 resolution engine, T05 dispatch/claims, T07 seed parser — and Opus for the mechanical ones (T03 days, T06 sprints); implementers are Opus everywhere (a good plan + codex diff review carries implementation); orchestrators always Fable.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5663:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1125:    39	Mechanical verification is delegated too (owner directive): Opus verification agents run `./verify` and smoke checks, archive the full output under `orchestration/verify-runs/`, and report headlines; the top-level agent runs checks itself only when truly cheap. The final goal demonstration is the exception — run fresh by the top-level agent with full output shown, as the goal condition requires.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5664:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1129:    43	SPEC §18.2's build check runs `node --check` on every file in `assets/`; `node --check` only parses JavaScript, and `assets/` legitimately holds CSS (`tokens.css`, required by §10). The instrument checks every `*.js` under `assets/` recursively — the only reading under which the required CSS can exist at all. Flagged by codex during the T02 implementation review; recorded here for the auditor.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5665:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1133:    47	T02 codex diff review: NO VIOLATIONS (seven confirmations, file/line cited; archive at orchestration/tickets/T02-verify/impl-review.md). T01 codex diff review found no missing enum members, tables, or route gaps; its closing schema cross-check (every table's columns vs its contract dataclass) succeeded inside the codex session and was independently re-run by the top-level orchestrator: all six audited tables match exactly, all ten tables present (archive at orchestration/tickets/T01-contracts/impl-review.md). Verify run 001 (orchestration/verify-runs/001-stage2.md) matched the stage-2 expectation exactly: gates green, 36 FAIL, `VERIFY: 0/36 PASS`, exit 1.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5667:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1180:    11	A database-backed personal planning system replacing a markdown-file system. It manages sprints, sprint items, tickets, and planning days for a single user (Khushal), and lets AI agents take tickets through a shaping-and-execution pipeline under explicit per-ticket permission ceilings. It runs entirely on the local machine.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5668:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1182:    13	The system is: one SQLite database; one Python server process (JSON API + web UI + WebSocket event feed + embedded dispatcher + boundary scheduler); one CLI used by agents (the human operates through the UI, never the CLI); a set of skill documents as artifacts. Agents never mutate canonical state directly — they write proposals; code resolves them.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5669:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1191:    22	- Layout: `src/planner/` organised by semantic domain — `core/` (shared infrastructure: db, events, config, server shell, adapters' interfaces) plus `tickets/`, `sprints/`, `days/`, `dispatch/`, `seed/`, `chat/`. Within every domain, organise by kind of work (contracts, logic, data, api) — layers within domains, never feature-slices, never one flat pile. Non-trivial things get folders.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5670:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1213:    44	- Fields: `title`, `state` (Section 4.1), `priority` ∈ {P0..P3} (default P3), `deadline` (nullable date), `project` (same enum, nullable when parented — derived from parent's item), `sprint_item_id` (nullable), `sprint_id` (nullable; writable only when `sprint_item_id` is NULL — writes rejected otherwise; when parented it is derived from the parent item), `recap` (markdown, writable only when state is past `needs_success`; writes before that are rejected with a structured error), `ceiling` (a ticket state; Section 4.3), `at_cap` ∈ {`stop`, `propose`}, `auto_blocked` (bool, Section 7.5), `chat_session_key` (nullable string), `fields` (Section 4.2).
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5672:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1274:   105	- **Pickup queue** (dispatcher eligibility): Section 7.2.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5673:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1290:   121	Runs once per planning date, at the first scheduler tick at/after the boundary hour (and never twice for the same date; a `boundary_runs` table records completions). It has a deterministic pass (pure code) and a judgment pass (agent adapter):
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5674:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1307:   138	- Invalidating the root sets root and every child `invalidated` and triggers one replan-adapter call that returns a complete new tree (replacing the old; old tree preserved in the event payload). Invalidating a child replans only that node: the adapter returns a replacement child. Replans serialize; if an invalidation arrives while a replan is in flight, the in-flight result is discarded and one new replan runs (latest wins) [RULING R5 default].
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5675:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1310:   141	## 7. The dispatcher and runs
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5676:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1314:   145	Every 60s (configurable), inside the server process. Order within a tick: (1) reclaim expired claims and detect dead worker processes; (2) recompute eligibility; (3) spawn up to the concurrency cap. A machine-wide advisory file lock (`data/dispatcher.lock`) guarantees a single dispatcher; a `dispatch_enabled` config flag is re-read every tick and fails safe to `false` on config read error.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5677:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1318:   149	A ticket is eligible iff ALL of: not `done`/`dropped`/`needs_review`; not blocked (3.6); not `auto_blocked`; no active claim; and an agent can do work on it — meaning the current gating field has no pending proposal AND either (a) the advance-target ≤ `ceiling` (the agent can move it), or (b) the ticket is at its ceiling with `at_cap = propose` (the agent can draft the gating field for approval). A ticket at its ceiling with `at_cap = stop` is never eligible. Ordering: priority (P0 first), then deadline ascending with NULLs last, then `created_at` ascending.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5678:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1320:   151	### 7.3 Claims and runs
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5679:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1322:   153	- Claim = CAS: `UPDATE tickets SET claim_lock=?, claim_expires=? WHERE id=? AND claim_lock IS NULL`; rowcount 0 = lost. TTL 15 minutes. Every claim inserts a `runs` row (`ticket_id`, `status` ∈ {running, done, blocked, crashed, timed_out, reclaimed}, `started_at`, `ended_at`, `summary`, `error`, `pid`).
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5680:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1323:   154	- Heartbeat (CLI verb) extends `claim_expires` by one TTL. Expired claim or dead PID → run closed `reclaimed`, lock cleared.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5681:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1324:   155	- Max concurrent runs: 2 [RULING R7 default]. Per-run max runtime: 30 minutes → SIGTERM, run `timed_out`.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5682:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1328:   159	Through a **spawn adapter** (subprocess boundary, mockable): `hermes -p <profile> --skills <skill> chat -q "work planning ticket <id>"`, detached session, per-run log file under `data/logs/`, env: `PLAN_SERVER_URL`, `PLAN_TICKET_ID`, `PLAN_RUN_ID`, `PLAN_CLAIM`. Profile and skill names come from config [RULING R3 default: profile `default`, skill `planning-worker`]. Tests never spawn real processes.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5683:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1332:   163	A run ending `crashed`, `timed_out`, or `spawn_failed` increments `tickets.consecutive_failures`; a run ending `done` resets it to 0. Reaching 2 sets `auto_blocked = true` (sticky; event `auto_blocked`). The dispatcher never picks up an auto-blocked ticket; a human clear-action resets both flag and counter.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5684:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1336:   167	Every agent write (proposal, recap, heartbeat, run close) must carry `PLAN_RUN_ID` + `PLAN_CLAIM` matching the ticket's active claim, except proposal/recap writes from non-dispatched agent contexts (no claim env — e.g. the planner-main chat agent), which are permitted with the proposer taken from `PLAN_ACTOR` (default `agent`). Human writes happen through the UI/API only — the CLI is an agent tool. A write with a stale/foreign claim is rejected with a structured error naming the mismatch.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5685:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1340:   171	A single entry point (`plan`, installed via `pip install -e .` console script) speaking HTTP to the server. **The CLI is for agents (and developer debugging); the human never uses it — every human action lives in the UI.** No resolution verbs exist in it: accepting, approving, granting, unblocking, and day-plan decisions are UI/API-only. Global behavior: `--json` on every verb (machine output; stable exit codes: 0 success, 1 validation/domain error, 2 connection error); long text via `--body-file <path>` or `-` for stdin — body text is never passed as an inline argument; env defaults `PLAN_SERVER_URL`, `PLAN_TICKET_ID` (verbs taking a ticket id use it when omitted), `PLAN_RUN_ID`, `PLAN_CLAIM`, `PLAN_ACTOR`.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5686:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1354:   185	- REST JSON under `/api/`: CRUD + actions mirroring Section 8 verbs, PLUS the human actions that deliberately have no CLI form — accept/edit-accept with the grant pair, `needs_review` approve, unblock, grant changes, sprint create/freeze, day-plan accept/accept-all/invalidate/reject-all, drop — and `GET /api/board`, `GET /api/day/<date>`, `GET /api/sprint/current`, `GET /api/queues`. Every mutating endpoint routes through the same canonical functions (one writer per edge).
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5687:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1356:   187	- Test mode (Section 13): `POST /api/test/tick-boundary` and `POST /api/test/tick-dispatcher` run one tick synchronously; available only when test mode is on, else 404.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5688:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1388:   219	- `config.yaml` at repo root (checked in with defaults) + env overrides: `PLAN_DB_PATH` (default `data/planning.db`), `PLAN_PORT` (8767), `PLAN_BOUNDARY_HOUR` (5), `PLAN_TICK_SECONDS` (60), `PLAN_CLAIM_TTL_SECONDS` (900), `PLAN_MAX_RUNS` (2), `PLAN_FAILURE_LIMIT` (2), `PLAN_DISPATCH_ENABLED` (true), `PLAN_HERMES_BIN`, `PLAN_HERMES_PROFILE`, `PLAN_WORKER_SKILL`.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5689:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1408:   239	Defaults the owner may override before the run; each is tagged where it binds: **R1** boundary hour 05:00. **R2** new-ticket grant `ceiling=needs_success, at_cap=propose`. **R3** spawn profile `default`, worker skill `planning-worker`. **R4** boundary evidence DB-internal only. **R5** replan serialization, latest-wins. **R6** seed excludes archives. **R7** max concurrent runs 2.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5691:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1418:   249	**Build in this order**: (1) contracts skeleton — schema DDL, enums, typed models, adapter interfaces, config loading, CLI/API surface stubs; (2) **the verify instrument** (18.2), runnable with every checklist item FAIL; (3) pure-logic modules + unit tests (items 1–21 and 36) green; (4) server wiring — API, WS, resolution engine over DB, dispatcher, boundary scheduler, CLI against the API; (5) UI views; (6) Playwright e2e suite, items 22–34 green; (7) the fake-ticket dogfood pass (Section 18.5): Level A green (item 35), Levels B and C executed with evidence recorded in DOGFOOD.md. Do not start a later stage while an earlier stage's tests fail.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5692:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1426:   257	- **DOCS.md** — plain-language documentation written progressively as each stage completes, never retrofitted. A smart non-engineer must be able to read it: what the planner is, the entities, how proposals and ceilings work, what the dispatcher does, how a day flows, what each screen shows, what the CLI verbs do, what the skills are for. If a section needs the code to be understood, rewrite the section.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5693:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1434:   265	- Unit items are 1–21 and 36; end-to-end items are 22–35. Item 34 (snapshot migration) runs against `migration/source-snapshot/`, and item 35 (dogfood Level A) is the scripted walkthrough — both part of the suite, not manual steps. Levels B and C of the dogfood (Section 18.5) are deliberately OUTSIDE `./verify`: they use live agents and belong to stage 7's evidence, not the deterministic gate.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5695:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1460:   291	14. Claim CAS: two concurrent claim attempts on one ticket → exactly one wins, one `runs` row created.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5696:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1462:   293	16. Circuit breaker: two consecutive `crashed` runs → `auto_blocked`, ineligible; unblock action clears flag and counter; a `done` run resets the counter.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5697:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1478:   309	30. Dispatcher e2e: eligible ticket + fake spawn adapter; `POST /api/test/tick-dispatcher` claims it (run visible on Ticket); the fake worker's CLI calls (propose result with claim env) park it in `needs_review`; it appears in the approval queue; approve → `done`.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5698:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1483:   314	35. Dogfood Level A: `scripts/dogfood_cli.py` drives the full workflow against a test server seeded with `plan seed --demo` — agent actions through the real CLI, human actions (accepts with grant pairs, needs_review approve, day-plan decisions) through the HTTP API, since the CLI deliberately has no human verbs: create a ticket; propose and accept through every gate — every accept carrying its grant pair — including one edit-accept and one superseded proposal; write and overwrite the recap; assign to the day and remove; create a blocking link and confirm dispatch-ineligibility, then complete the blocker and confirm eligibility; claim via the test dispatcher tick; propose result with claim env; approve from needs_review to done. The script asserts each step's resulting state via `--json`/response output and exits non-zero on the first mismatch.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5699:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1492:   323	- **Level C — a dispatched ticket agent end to end.** Enable the real spawn adapter (config), one eligible demo ticket, real dispatcher tick: claim → run row → the spawned agent writes at least one proposal carrying the claim env → run closes with a terminal outcome and the ticket lands where its ceiling dictates. Evidence: the run row, the event sequence, and the per-run log path, all in DOGFOOD.md.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5700:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1494:   325	Levels B and C depend on live agent behavior: apply the three-attempt rule per level (materially different approach each time — prompt shape, ticket shaping, ceiling), and record every attempt honestly in DOGFOOD.md. Completion requires both levels' mechanical evidence. If server, CLI, or dispatcher code changes after a level's evidence was produced, that level is stale and must be re-run.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5701:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/impl-review-raw.txt:1498:   329	Live Hermes gateway chat; live dispatcher spawning a real agent; the final cutover seed against the live `~/.hermes/planning/` at switch time (the in-run migration proves the machinery on the frozen snapshot; the live directory will have drifted since 2026-07-04 and its import is reviewed by the human); installing skills into `~/.hermes/skills/`; launchd supervision; UI look-and-feel judgment.
