./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5820:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/plan-review-raw.txt:2389:orchestration/tickets/T09-server-shell/ticket.md:24:Self-smoke (scripted, not committed as tests — e2e items cover this later): boot under test mode on a temp DB; `GET /api/meta` 200; WS `?since=0` receives an event appended via `append_event`; `POST /api/test/set-now` changes the planning date returned by a probe; stale-claim rejection unit-exercisable by calling `require_claim` directly. ruff + mypy strict clean; existing unit suite stays green.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5821:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/plan-review-raw.txt:2485:decisions.md:43:SPEC §18.2's build check runs `node --check` on every file in `assets/`; `node --check` only parses JavaScript, and `assets/` legitimately holds CSS (`tokens.css`, required by §10). The instrument checks every `*.js` under `assets/` recursively — the only reading under which the required CSS can exist at all. Flagged by codex during the T02 implementation review; recorded here for the auditor.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5822:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/plan-review-raw.txt:2498:SPEC.md:11:A database-backed personal planning system replacing a markdown-file system. It manages sprints, sprint items, tickets, and planning days for a single user (Khushal), and lets AI agents take tickets through a shaping-and-execution pipeline under explicit per-ticket permission ceilings. It runs entirely on the local machine.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5823:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/plan-review-raw.txt:2499:SPEC.md:13:The system is: one SQLite database; one Python server process (JSON API + web UI + WebSocket event feed + embedded dispatcher + boundary scheduler); one CLI used by agents (the human operates through the UI, never the CLI); a set of skill documents as artifacts. Agents never mutate canonical state directly — they write proposals; code resolves them.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5824:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/plan-review-raw.txt:2508:SPEC.md:44:- Fields: `title`, `state` (Section 4.1), `priority` ∈ {P0..P3} (default P3), `deadline` (nullable date), `project` (same enum, nullable when parented — derived from parent's item), `sprint_item_id` (nullable), `sprint_id` (nullable; writable only when `sprint_item_id` is NULL — writes rejected otherwise; when parented it is derived from the parent item), `recap` (markdown, writable only when state is past `needs_success`; writes before that are rejected with a structured error), `ceiling` (a ticket state; Section 4.3), `at_cap` ∈ {`stop`, `propose`}, `auto_blocked` (bool, Section 7.5), `chat_session_key` (nullable string), `fields` (Section 4.2).
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5825:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/plan-review-raw.txt:2549:SPEC.md:257:- **DOCS.md** — plain-language documentation written progressively as each stage completes, never retrofitted. A smart non-engineer must be able to read it: what the planner is, the entities, how proposals and ceilings work, what the dispatcher does, how a day flows, what each screen shows, what the CLI verbs do, what the skills are for. If a section needs the code to be understood, rewrite the section.
./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5826:orchestration/tickets/T14-ui-foundation/plan-review-raw.txt:2566:SPEC.md:314:35. Dogfood Level A: `scripts/dogfood_cli.py` drives the full workflow against a test server seeded with `plan seed --demo` — agent actions through the real CLI, human actions (accepts with grant pairs, needs_review approve, day-plan decisions) through the HTTP API, since the CLI deliberately has no human verbs: create a ticket; propose and accept through every gate — every accept carrying its grant pair — including one edit-accept and one superseded proposal; write and overwrite the recap; assign to the day and remove; create a blocking link and confirm dispatch-ineligibility, then complete the blocker and confirm eligibility; claim via the test dispatcher tick; propose result with claim env; approve from needs_review to done. The script asserts each step's resulting state via `--json`/response output and exits non-zero on the first mismatch.
