2886-orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6033:PROGRESS.md:49:Stage 6: dispatch T18 (e2e harness + items 22–27, Fable planner) → when its conftest lands, T19 (items 28–32) ∥ T20 (items 33–34). Then delegated verify (expect 35/36 — item 35 needs T21), stage-6 commit. Stage 7: T21 (dogfood script + four skills docs) closes item 35 → verify 36/36; then Levels B/C run TOP-LEVEL with live hermes (evidence into DOGFOOD.md; staleness rule: rerun if server/CLI/dispatcher code changes after). Then final sequence in ONE turn: fresh ./verify full output → codex audit (codex exec --model gpt-5.6 with codex-audit.md contents) → PASS or fix-loop. DOCS.md needs stage-6/7 sections + a CLI-verbs/skills summary before the audit. Known hazards: codex exec hangs (kill + relaunch tighter); orchestrators idle mid-pipeline (ping them); session restarts kill sub-agents (respawn closers against inherited state); e2e anchored-test 1:1 fence. Anomaly (historic): an unowned codex process ran the AUDIT prompt before the 13:00 restart — void, ignore.
2887:orchestration/tickets/T21-dogfood-skills/codex-plan-review-raw.txt:6035:DOCS.md:40:- **Stage 4 — the server (done):** the rules now answer the door. One local process serves a JSON interface for every action in the system, and it enforces who may do what: requests from a working agent must carry a valid claim on the ticket they're touching (a stale or borrowed claim is refused, and the refusal names what was wrong without ever revealing the live claim itself); the actions reserved for the human — approving, granting, unblocking, dropping, freezing, deciding the day plan — refuse agents entirely. A live feed announces every change so screens can refresh themselves; the feed is only a doorbell, never the data. The dispatcher and the morning boundary job now actually run on their schedules (in testing they run only when explicitly ticked, so tests are exact). The command-line tool agents use is complete: every verb talks to the server, returns clean machine-readable output, and deliberately contains no approval powers at all. Four review findings worth naming, all caught by the independent reviewer and fixed: a path that leaked the live claim token into an error detail; a check that ran validation before verifying the claim; a number that could sneak into a date field untyped; and an environment value that could trick the tool into reading the wrong input.
